{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788291461,"nonce":"vSV8PpusFOZJE8L4+5X5zf3v3bk=","message":"SHIPPED by session 19, branch `spec/until-condition` (commit 5e94e63), proven\nlive. `Source/AutoRimmer/StateWatch.cs` is the new file; `TimeDriver` gains one\nenum value and one poll.\n\n## THE HEADLINE, from a raw envelope rather than a suite's green number\n\n    accfc287ba-061-advance.json   reason=layout   ticks_elapsed=10255\n      halted_on {\"layout_id\":\"ly-5\",\"elements\":22,\"built\":22,\"cancelled\":0,\n                 \"resolved\":22,\"unresolved\":0,\"done\":true,\"kind\":\"layout\"}\n      until     {..., \"every_frames\":15, \"evaluations\":114,\n                 \"eval_ms_avg\":0.0131, \"eval_ms_per_frame\":0.0008}\n\nOne call. No tick count anywhere in it, only a timeout bound. `rooms` afterwards:\n`role: \"Bedroom\"`, `proper: true`, `cells: 15`, `open_roof_cells: 0`.\n\n## Comment #3's open question, SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT ON THE BENCH\n\nPhase 4 does not argue the false-at-start case, it measures it. At the moment\nthe 22 blueprints go down:\n\n    digest.construction.frames      == 0      (check 4.3a)\n    digest.construction.blueprints  == 22     (check 4.3b)\n    construction{layout_id}.unresolved == 22  (check 4.3c)\n\nSo `construction.frames == 0` is TRUE on a room that does not exist, on the\nsame tick the layout scope reports nothing resolved. A path predicate halts\nthere and reports success. **And an edge does not save it** — the ~900-tick\nawaiting-materials window this issue records is a real false→true→false→true\nsequence.\n\nThat is why the layout form is a NAMED FAMILY and not a path, and it settles\ncomment #3's stronger claim: **some predicates are not expressible as an\noperator over a digest path at all.** The monotone claim lives in\n`Placements.StateOf` and is a source reading, cited there: blueprint and frame\ninterchange freely (`Frame.FailConstruction` destroys the frame and spawns the\nblueprint again) but neither ever comes back from `built` or `cancelled`. Both\nhalves of that failure run inside ONE TICK and this polls once per frame from\n`GameComponentUpdate`, so there is no interleaving to catch.\n\n## The verification comment's three corrections, all honoured\n\n**Correction 1 (`food_days \u003c 3` is LATER than the alert).** Not used as the\nworked example. The arithmetic is now a comment on `DigestVerb.ResourceSection`\nwhere a caller will actually meet it, citing both members:\n`Alert_LowFood.MapWithLowFood` trips at `TotalHumanEdibleNutrition \u003c 4f *\nFreeColonistsSpawnedCount` while `GetExplanation` divides by colonists PLUS\nprisoners and the digest mirrors the explanation — so a threshold meant to LEAD\nmust sit above 4. The real leading argument is recorded there too:\n`GetReport` opens `if (TicksGame \u003c 150000f) return false;`, so for 2.5 in-game\ndays the alert cannot fire however empty the larder is.\n\n**Correction 2 (the budget is gone).** Cadence is in FRAMES,\n`Config.ConditionScanFrames = 15`, counted the way `AlertScanner` counts them.\nCost is reported as ms/frame because there is no denominator any more. And the\ncomment's best news was right: `TimeDriver.Step` already halts on three state\nfacts, so this is one more poll at an existing site, not new machinery.\n**Measured:** `time.hour` 0.0219 ms/eval, **0.0015 ms/frame**; a 22-element\nlayout 0.0131 ms/eval, **0.0008 ms/frame**. Against the journal's own\n0.0039 ms/frame through DPA, a predicate costs a quarter to a half of what the\njournal already costs.\n\n**Correction 3 (`colonists[*]` does not parse).** `colonists.list[*].mood_pct`\nis the addressable spelling, and the issue's own example is a REFUSAL that says\nso: check 0.6f sends `colonists[*].mood_pct` and gets \"'colonists' is not a\nlist, so `[*]` cannot be applied to it\". The head segment is split for brackets\nexactly like every other segment, so the message names the real problem instead\nof \"not a digest section\".\n\n**Miss 1 (the 204-tick floor).** Stated in `StateWatch.cs`'s header and in\n`Config`, and published per-advance as `until.floor_note` on any `resources.*`\npredicate. Not enforced: the cadence is in frames and the floor is in ticks, and\nthe conversion moves with the speed.\n\n**Miss 2 (read cost is not uniform).** Only the ONE section a path names is\nbuilt, never the whole digest — `time` is nine field reads, `colonists` is a\n`Room.Role` per colonist. `DigestVerb.SectionFor` carries the argument.\n\n**Miss 3 (you cannot cross-check from inside the advance).** True and load\nbearing, so `halted_on` is the sole evidence and it carries the path, the\noperator, the value asked for, the value observed and the tick. The suite checks\nit post-hoc against an independent `construction {layout_id}` read.\n\n## Comment #2's clock, which the body never mentioned\n\n`{path:\"time.hour\", op:\"==\", value:H+1}` halted 983 ticks in on the recorded\nrun, and `digest.time.hour` afterwards agreed (3.3e). **The EDGE is proved both\nways on the same predicate**, which is the cleanest demonstration available:\n`hour \u003e= 0` is true always, so with the edge required it exits on\n`timeout_ticks` reporting `true_when_armed: true, saw_false: false` (3.1h-k),\nand with `edge:false` the identical predicate halts in 42 ticks (3.2a).\n\"Wait until dawn\" issued at 14:00 does not return instantly.\n\n## Acceptance, item by item\n\n- **\"halts within one cadence window of the crossing, reports the path and the\n  tripping value\"** — 3.3a-c. `every_frames: 15` is published, so the window is\n  stated rather than assumed.\n- **\"halts EARLIER than the corresponding vanilla alert\"** — NOT ATTEMPTED, and\n  deliberately. The verification showed it fails as written and cannot be armed\n  anyway, since `until` takes one matcher. It needs a colony driven into\n  starvation across 2.5 in-game days on a fixed seed — a play-loop fixture, not\n  a placement one. Named here rather than quietly dropped.\n- **\"a predicate that is never true exits on `timeout_ticks`, not by hanging\"** —\n  5.3a, and it does strictly better than the criterion asks: `until` carries\n  `unresolved_items` with every outstanding element and its state\n  (`awaiting-materials` for a layout of Plasteel on a map with none), which is\n  the diagnosis a fixed-tick advance never gave.\n- **\"evaluation cost measured\"** — above, restated as ms/frame per Correction 2.\n- **\"no mutation observable\"** — restated as the project's real invariant in\n  `StateWatch.cs`'s header: nothing SCRIBED changes and the shared `Rand` stream\n  is untouched. `threats.danger` and `colonists.list[*].room` do write\n  (`dangerRatingInt`, `role`/`stats`), idempotently and RNG-free — the same\n  ruling `CostListCalculator`'s cache already got. The criterion as written\n  would either forbid two useful paths or be vacuous.\n- **\"zero red errors\"** — 5.5.\n- Comment #3's two extra criteria: 4.4a-f (halts on completion, does NOT halt at\n  t=0 despite `construction.frames == 0`) and 5.3-5.4 (timeout names the\n  unresolved placements and their states).\n\n## The guard rail this round added, which the issue did not ask for\n\n`until` was a `ContainsKey` else-if chain: a second matcher was silently\noutranked and a misspelled one silently ignored, so `until:{conditon:{…}}` armed\nNOTHING and presented as an advance that ran to its timeout for no stated\nreason. That is `7382bdd`'s class with ten in-game days attached. Now: exactly\none matcher, no unknown keys, and the predicate is evaluated ONCE at arm time —\nwhich validates the path (naming the keys the section really publishes) and\nseeds the edge. Eight refusals are asserted, and check 0.7 verifies the clock\ndid not move across any of them, since every one happens after `TimeDriver.Start`\nhas already set the game's speed.\n\n`halted_seq` is now published only when it names a journal line. A state halt\nhas none, and a 0 would send a caller to `journal --since 0`.\n\n## Where this extends, and what was deliberately NOT closed\n\nResearch, bills, plant growth and `61794cd`'s bleed clock are all the same gap.\nNone is closed here. The surface is shaped so closing them later is a\n`StateWatch` subclass and one parse branch: anything the digest publishes is\nalready reachable by path, and anything that is a NAMED COMMITMENT rather than\na global reading is a class beside `LayoutWatch`. `TimeDriver` does not have to\nlearn what any of them mean — `StateWatch.MatcherKeys`/`OptionKeys` is the\nvocabulary and it lives in that file.\n\nFour branches of what shipped are NOT covered by the 140 green checks and are\nfiled as `039e359`: `Materials.unreachable`, a `colonists.list[*]` predicate\n(the expensive path — so the one number this spec asks to be measured is\nmeasured only on the cheapest path available), `scan_cap`/`unscanned`, and the\n`unknown`/`off_map` branches.","files":null}]}